  "nodes": [
    {
      "id": "22d4a0db-c910-4c96-b55f-025a3f195680",
      "name": "Sticky Note",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        0,
        0
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "width": 480,
        "height": 1088,
        "content": "## AI Deployment Window Optimizer\n\n### How it works\n\nThis workflow receives a deployment request, enriches it with configuration fields, and uses an AI agent to recommend an optimal deployment window based on incident history, team availability, traffic, and past deployment failures. It logs the recommendation, checks whether the risk is acceptable, and either posts a high-risk notice or requests deployment approval. If approved, it creates a calendar hold, schedules the deployment through a CI/CD API, and posts confirmation; if not approved, it posts a reschedule notice.\n\n### Setup steps\n\n- Configure the webhook URL and expected request payload fields for service, change description, version, and requester.\n- Add OpenAI credentials for the chat model used by the AI agent.\n- Configure the AI agent tool HTTP requests for incident history, team availability, traffic analytics, and deployment failure history endpoints.\n- Set up Postgres credentials and ensure the recommendation logging table/schema matches the fields written by the workflow.\n- Connect Slack credentials and choose the channels/users for approval requests, high-risk notices, reschedule notices, and scheduled confirmations.\n- Connect Google Calendar credentials and select the calendar where deployment holds should be created.\n- Configure the CI/CD HTTP request endpoint, authentication, and payload for scheduling deployments.\n\n### Customization\n\nAdjust the AI prompt, risk threshold, approval routing, calendar event details, and CI/CD scheduling payload to match internal deployment policies."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "bd022b6d-8404-4eca-bcf7-8dd34f6fc691",
      "name": "Sticky Note1",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
